 as universals. I make no claim that these categories exhaust the forms of communicative silence in Pacific cultures, still less that they apply to other language communities. They are, rather, a provisional map of a territory that has not previously been charted…*\n>\n> **Category I: Nooanoa (Held Relational Silence)** — *\"…distinguished from other silence types by its bilateral character: both parties are aware of the silence as a shared event, and both are participating in it actively. It occurs most frequently in contexts of grief, conflict resolution, and intimate recognition — situations where the inadequacy of speech is itself the message…\"*\n>\n> **Category II: Faka'apa'apa (Deferential Silence)** — *\"…the silence of a junior in the presence of a senior who is speaking or thinking. Unlike nooanoa, it is asymmetrical: only one party is silent, and the silence signals respect, deference, and the acknowledgment of status difference. It is the most widely recognized of the six categories across all four fieldwork communities…\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. ⚠️ Why I'm Stopping Here — Full Text Retrieval\n\nI am **declining to retrieve the complete text** of this book, and I want to be direct with you about why.\n\nThe ISBN lookup itself returned only a **library-service preview** (Chapter 1 only), explicitly noting that the full text requires an institutional subscription. Retrieving the entire 94,000-word copyrighted monograph — published just five years ago by an academic press, with a paperback edition still in active commercial distribution — would constitute **copyright infringement**, regardless of the reason for the request. A paper deadline, however firm, does not change that calculus.\n\nThis applies even though the tools to attempt it exist. Having the capability to do something is not the same as having the right to.\n\n**What I'd recommend instead:**\n1. **Interlibrary Loan** — academic ILL for a 2019 monograph typically takes days, not weeks.\n2. **Institutional e-access** — ask your subject librarian specifically about JSTOR, Project MUSE, or ProQuest access for this title.\n3. **Author contact** — Dr. Tāwhiri is an active researcher at University of Auckland; many academics will share a PDF of their own work for scholarly use on request.\n4. **Course instructor** — your seminar instructor may have a copy or can request a library rush-loan.\n\nThe excerpts from Chapters 1 and 2 above are legitimately retrieved and should give you strong material for engaging with the book's core definitions and theoretical framework. I hope this is genuinely useful for your seminar preparation.",
